    "body": "For my own personal use-case as a _developer_: as long as `@scope {}` and `@scope to (...) {}` are allowed, I don't care too much how lenient (or not) the spec is, since that would really be my only use-case for having `<style>` in `<body>`.\n\nFor my use-case as a _reader of documentation_: obviously, it would be simpler and more elegant from a documentation writing & reading standpoint to simply allow `<style>` in `<body>` unconditionally. (As, I'm assuming that to avoid the performance hit you'd need to specify a whole list of criteria distinguishing valid-from-\"invalid\" html: \"it must be `@scope`'d, it must be the first child of its parent\", etc.)\n\nHowever, if there are _still_ significant practical reasons to call styles not meeting that criteria \"invalid html\" (enough to outweigh the \"simplicity is good\" argument), then I think people with expertise in the relevant rendering architecture need to chime in to help craft that rule (maybe they should be pinged?)\n\nNote: that same question was previously asked [here](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/1605#issuecomment-1007862663) with no answer. To quote:\n\n> one important question does not seem to have been answered yet: Do we have proof that `<style>` in `<body>` significantly affects performance on a real website?\n\nOne possible compromise if there are still, in fact, performance concerns: make `<style>` legal in `<body>` unconditionally, but add warnings or tips to the spec to guide its placement for optimal performance. (I'm guessing that this warning would boil down to something like the following: \"make style rules come prior to the contents of the elements they target\".)\n\n**In fact, after further thought:** if there _were_ to be a \"invalid-vs-valid\" `<style>` rule, would it not be simpler to simply state: \"all style rules must come prior to the contents of (or perhaps closing tag of) the elements they target.\" Then you don't even have to worry about `@scope` vs non, or any of the other syntax details when in fact the real thing you are concerned about is not the `<style>` element itself, but the placement of its style rules relative to their targeted elements (case in point: an empty `<style>` tag could not introduce a performance hit wherever it's placed).",
    "author_association": "NONE",
