    "created_at": "2026-09-16T21:29:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-09-16T21:29:02Z",
    "body": "> Do we have proof that `<style>` in `<body>` significantly affects performance on a real website?\n\nChanging the style of an earlier element or an ancestor causes a style invalidation and may need recalculating layout. How bad that is depends on the page. If the earlier content was rendered before the style is applied it means the user sees a FOUC, which can be jarring. There are other ways to get the same result (e.g. changing a class name on `html` or `body`), but `<style>` in `<body>` is still a footgun that conformance checkers can check for.\n\n> \"all style rules must come prior to the contents of (or perhaps closing tag of) the elements they target.\"\n\nYeah, we could say that a selector that matches anything before the `style` element's parent in tree order is \"should not\" or \"must not\". (I assume there are use cases for styling the parent, which could cause a style invalidation, but it's just one element.)",
